WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:05.040
So the idea was that in looking at thin sections of yeast cells,

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:10.190
one can see some organelles that look like they could be associated with 

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:13.670
secretion, not as dramatic as in a section of mammalian cells,

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:19.110
but Novick and I guessed that the vesicles that one sees congregating under the bud portion of a dividing 

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:22.120
cell would be the vehicle not only for secretion,

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:23.710
but for assembly of the plasma membrane.

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:28.790
And so we predicted that anything that blocked the biogenesis of these vesicles would be 

00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:30.670
lethal, and that therefore,

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:34.410
one would have to go after conditional, temperature sensitive, lethal mutations.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:39.660
So that was the first and crucial assumption that it was going to be an essential 

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:40.350
gene.

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:43.880
And the second was that the mutant would,

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:45.710
at a restrictive temperature,

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:50.510
cause secretory proteins, which are otherwise very efficiently secreted from cells, to 

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:55.160
be accumulated inside of the cell. And that actually really trivialized it,

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:57.970
you just assay intact cells,

00:00:57.970 --> 00:01:00.760
which measures the enzyme that has been trapped in the cell wall,

00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:01.770
the cell walls porous.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:05.950
And then you dissolve the cell wall with an enzyme and you have spheroplasts 

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:11.760
 that are left and then you just rupture the spheroplasts with a detergent and you assay what's inside the spheroplast.

00:01:11.990 --> 00:01:16.750
And that's a measure of accumulation. In a wild type cell, that baseline is virtually 

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:19.200
zero. In a sec mutant,

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:21.340
it rises dramatically above the background.

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:22.490
So the first mutant,

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:25.810
just based on a biochemical enzyme assay,

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:30.800
was really obviously blocked in multiple secretory enzyme transport.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:35.870
But the dramatic result came just around the time George Palade visited Berkeley for a 

00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:36.750
special lecture,

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:41.820
and I recall Novick telling me that at the luncheon Palade had encouraged him with these 

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:43.680
preliminary results to cut a thin section,

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:45.050
to look and see what these cells looked like.

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:49.650
And so we had an expert technical assistant in the department.

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:50.470
And that,

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:50.790
of course,

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:55.790
was one of the great eureka moments of my career when Novick excitedly 

00:01:55.790 --> 00:02:00.640
called me down to the EM room and showed me these sec1 cell sections 

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:03.080
that were just like they had measles,

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:03.950
they were just full of vesicles.

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:07.460
Nothing like that had ever-- at least never been published--

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:09.250
I don't think it had ever been seen before.

00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:10.950
And from that moment,

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:13.190
literally from that moment on,

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:17.300
I knew that we would have at least several decades worth of productive work. 

